  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively point in the right direction: arc melting and quenching can control phase constitution in Fe-Co-Ni-containing HEAs; compositional modification can produce Laves-containing multiphase alloys; and phase separation in magnetic HEAs can lower saturation magnetization while increasing coercivity. R18 is especially suggestive because it combines an antiferromagnetic constituent with phase separation and enhanced coercivity. However, none discloses rapidly quenched FeCoNiTi with coexisting ferromagnetic fcc and C14 phases, the matched C14-dominant comparison composition, Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, the phase-specific magnetic transitions, or orbital/spin-canting-assisted domain-wall pinning. Thus, selecting this exact alloy, processing route, and mechanism would still require a substantial new idea."
